<!--
  Skeletal (fill-in) version of 2.4 The Binomial Series and Applications
  of Taylor Series.

  Definitions, theorems, summaries, and problem statements are kept;
  derivations and solutions are replaced by blank workspace boxes, each
  with a one-line statement of what has to be done.  Content mirrors
  ch-10-10.ptx of the full lecture notes; every xml:id here is prefixed
  with "skel-" so the two can coexist.

  Figures: Pascal's triangle is a simple array, so only blank space is
  left for the student to build it from the coefficients they have just
  computed.  The counting figure and the square-root plot are complex,
  so they are reproduced in full, as in the lecture notes.
-->
<section xml:id="sec-skel-series-binomial" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>The Binomial Series and Applications of Taylor Series</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Write down the binomial series for <m>(1+x)^m</m> and compute the
binomial coefficients <m>\binom{m}{k}</m> for any real power <m>m</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Explain why the series terminates when <m>m</m> is a non-negative
integer and is infinite otherwise.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Expand functions such as <m>(x+1)^5</m>, <m>(x+1)^{-2}</m>, and
<m>(x+1)^{1/2}</m> as binomial series.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<introduction>
<p>
We have already seen one of the applications of the Taylor series,
namely approximating a function by a polynomial of degree <m>n</m>. In
this section we study two more applications of the Taylor series: the
binomial series and estimating/evaluating non-elementary integrals.
</p>
<p>
<em>These are skeletal notes: the definitions, theorems, and problem
statements are given, and blank boxes are left wherever a derivation, a
solution, or a sketch belongs, so that you can fill them in by hand.</em>
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-binomial-series">
<title>Binomial Series</title>

<p>
Start with a positive integer exponent, where nothing beyond
multiplication is needed. Multiply out by hand.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.35,7.6) {$(1+x)^1 =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.35,5.7) {$(1+x)^2 =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.35,3.8) {$(1+x)^3 =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.35,1.9) {$(1+x)^4 =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box with four rows labelled one plus x to the first, second, third, and fourth powers, for multiplying out each expansion by hand.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
Two things to notice: how many terms does <m>(1+x)^n</m> have, and what
pattern do the coefficients follow? Strip the coefficients out and stack
one row per exponent, each row centered below the one before it.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-pascals-triangle">
<caption>
Pascal's triangle. Row <m>n</m> holds the coefficients of
<m>(1+x)^n</m>.
</caption>
<image width="97%">
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.65) {$n = 0$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.20) {$n = 1$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.75) {$n = 2$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.30) {$n = 3$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.85) {$n = 4$:};
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.35,1.20) {write each row centered below the previous one};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box with five rows labelled n equals zero through n equals four, in which to write the coefficients of one plus x to the n, each row centered below the previous one.</shortdescription>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
This array is called <term>Pascal's triangle</term>. Every row starts and
ends with <m>1</m>. State the rule that produces each remaining entry
from the row above it, use it to write row <m>n = 5</m>, and read off
<m>(1+x)^5</m>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4.5) -- (15,4.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {the rule:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.35,4.2) {row $n = 5$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.35,2.3) {$(1+x)^5 =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box with rows for the addition rule of Pascal's triangle, the fifth row of the triangle, and the resulting expansion of one plus x to the fifth power.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
The triangle tells us <em>what</em> the coefficients are, but not yet
<em>why</em>. Written out in full, the fourth power is

<md xml:id="eq-skel-fourth-power" number="yes">
(1+x)^4 = (1+x)(1+x)(1+x)(1+x),
</md>

and every term of the expansion comes from picking either the <m>1</m>
or the <m>x</m> out of each factor. So <m>x^3</m> needs the <m>x</m>
from three of the four factors and the <m>1</m> from the one left over.
List the ways, and read off the coefficient of <m>x^3</m>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,2.5) -- (15,2.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.7) {the ways to pick three $x$'s:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.2) {coefficient of $x^3$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two parts, one for listing the ways of choosing three of the four factors to supply an x, and one for the resulting coefficient of x cubed.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
In general the coefficient of <m>x^k</m> in <m>(1+x)^n</m> counts the
ways of choosing <m>k</m> of the <m>n</m> factors to supply an <m>x</m>.
That count is written <m>\binom{n}{k}</m>, read <q>n choose k</q>, and
equals

<md xml:id="eq-skel-binom-factorial" number="yes">
\binom{n}{k} = \frac{n!}{k!\,(n-k)!}.
</md>
</p>

<exercise xml:id="skel-checkpoint-binomial-addition-rule">
<title>Why Does the Addition Rule Hold?</title>
<statement>
<p>
The rule you wrote down for Pascal's triangle is a consequence of the
same count. Explain why
<m>\binom{n}{k} = \binom{n-1}{k-1} + \binom{n-1}{k}</m>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,6.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for explaining the addition rule for binomial coefficients.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
<hint>
<p>
A choice of <m>k</m> factors out of <m>n</m> either uses the last factor
or it does not. Count the two kinds of choice separately.
</p>
</hint>
</exercise>

<p>
Putting the count together with the expansion gives the
<term>Binomial Theorem</term>:

<md xml:id="eq-skel-binomial-theorem" number="yes">
(1+x)^n = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \binom{n}{k} x^k
= 1 + nx + \frac{n(n-1)}{2!}x^2 + \cdots + nx^{n-1} + x^n.
</md>
</p>

<p>
What if the exponent is <em>not</em> a positive integer <mdash/> say
<m>-2</m>, or <m>\frac12</m>? The factorial formula
<xref ref="eq-skel-binom-factorial"/> makes no sense there, but the
equivalent <q>falling product</q> form

<md xml:id="eq-skel-binom-falling" number="yes">
\binom{m}{k} = \frac{m(m-1)(m-2)\cdots(m-k+1)}{k!}
</md>

asks only that we start at <m>m</m> and multiply <m>k</m> numbers, each
one less than the previous, and that recipe works for <em>any</em> real
<m>m</m>. Use it to explain why the expansion stops in one case and not
in the other.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4.5) -- (15,4.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {$m$ a non-negative integer:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.2) {$m$ negative or fractional:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two halves, for explaining why the coefficients eventually vanish when m is a non-negative integer and never vanish otherwise.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
So a negative or fractional <m>m</m> gives an infinite power series
rather than a polynomial. Newton took that step; Taylor series justify
it. Check that the coefficients
<xref ref="eq-skel-binom-falling"/> predicts really are the Taylor
coefficients of <m>f(x) = (1+x)^m</m> at <m>x = 0</m>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.35,9.65) {$f(x) = (1+x)^m$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.35,8.05) {$f'(x) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.35,6.45) {$f''(x) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.35,4.85) {$f'''(x) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.35,3.25) {$f^{(k)}(x) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (7.60,9.65) {$f(0) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (7.60,8.05) {$f'(0) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (7.60,6.45) {$f''(0) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (7.60,4.85) {$f'''(0) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (7.60,3.25) {$f^{(k)}(0) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.35,1.65) {$\dfrac{f^{(k)}(0)}{k!} =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box with rows for the successive derivatives of one plus x to the m, their values at zero, and the resulting Taylor coefficient divided by k factorial.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
The resulting series

<md xml:id="eq-skel-binomial-taylor" number="yes">
1 + mx + \frac{m(m-1)}{2!}x^2 + \cdots
+ \frac{m(m-1)(m-2)\cdots(m-k+1)}{k!}x^k + \cdots
</md>

is known as the <term>binomial series</term>.
</p>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-summary-binomial-series">
<title>Binomial Series</title>
<p>
For <m>-1 \lt x \lt 1</m>,
<md xml:id="eq-skel-binomial-series" number="yes">
(1+x)^m = 1 + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \binom{m}{k} x^k
</md>
where
<md>
\binom{m}{1} = m, \qquad
\binom{m}{2} = \frac{m(m-1)}{2},
</md>
and
<md>
<mrow>\binom{m}{k} \amp = \frac{m!}{k!(m-k)!}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{m(m-1)(m-2)\cdots(m-k+2)(m-k+1)}{k!}
\quad\text{for } k \ge 3.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<p>
<term>Remark:</term> The binomial series
<xref ref="eq-skel-binomial-series"/> converges for <m>|x| \lt 1</m>. We
will not prove this in these notes.
</p>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-binomial-positive">
<title>The power is a positive integer</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use binomial series to write the function <m>f(x) = (x+1)^5</m> as a
series.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Apply <xref ref="eq-skel-binomial-series"/> with <m>m = 5</m>, writing
out the terms through <m>k = 7</m>. Then compute <m>\binom{5}{6}</m>, and
compare your answer with the expansion you got from Pascal's triangle.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\draw[gray!35] (0,8.5) -- (15,8.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,5) -- (15,5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.7) {the series with $m = 5$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.2) {$\binom{5}{6} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (5.00,8.2) {$\binom{5}{k}$ for $k > 5$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.7) {$(1+x)^5 =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the binomial series with m equal to five, the vanishing coefficients beyond k equals five, and the final expansion.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-binomial-negative">
<title>The power is a negative integer</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use binomial series to write the function <m>f(x) = (x+1)^{-2}</m> as a
series.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Apply <xref ref="eq-skel-binomial-series"/> with <m>m = -2</m>. In the
general coefficient every factor is negative, so pull the signs out.
State where the series converges.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\draw[gray!35] (0,11) -- (15,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6.5) -- (15,6.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.7) {$\binom{-2}{1} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (5.00,13.7) {$\binom{-2}{2} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.7) {$\binom{-2}{k} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.2) {$(1+x)^{-2} =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the first two binomial coefficients, the general coefficient, and the resulting series for one plus x to the negative two.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-binomial-fractional">
<title>The power is not an integer</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use binomial series to write the function <m>f(x) = (x+1)^{\frac12}</m>
as a series. Compute the five non-zero terms.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Apply <xref ref="eq-skel-binomial-series"/> with <m>m = \frac12</m>.
Only the first factor of each coefficient is positive.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.7) {$\binom{\frac12}{1} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.3) {$\binom{\frac12}{2} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.9) {$\binom{\frac12}{3} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.5) {$\binom{\frac12}{4} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {$(1+x)^{\frac12} =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box with four rows for the binomial coefficients of the one half power and a final row for the five term expansion.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
Check your answer against <xref ref="fig-skel-binomial-sqrt"/>, which
plots <m>y = \sqrt{1+x}</m> with the partial sums of this series on the
interval of convergence <m>-1 \lt x \lt 1</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-binomial-sqrt">
<caption>
The function <m>y = \sqrt{1+x}</m> and the partial sums of its binomial
series with two, three, and five terms. On the interval
<m>-1 \lt x \lt 1</m> the partial sums approach the function.
</caption>
<image width="78%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-binomial-sqrt">
<diagram dimensions="(400,280)" margins="12">
<definition>f(t) = (t, (1 + t)^(1/2))</definition>
<definition>q1(t) = (t, 1 + t/2)</definition>
<definition>q2(t) = (t, 1 + t/2 - t^2/8)</definition>
<definition>q4(t) = (t, 1 + t/2 - t^2/8 + t^3/16 - 5*t^4/128)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.3,-0.35,1.3,1.9)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="bs-q1" function="q1" domain="(-1.0,1.0)"
                  stroke="#E69F00" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="bs-q2" function="q2" domain="(-1.0,1.0)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="bs-q4" function="q4" domain="(-1.0,1.0)"
                  stroke="#009E73" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="bs-f" function="f" domain="(-0.998,1.0)"
                  stroke="black" thickness="3" dash="7 4"/>
<label at="bs-f-lab" anchor="(-1.22,1.78)" alignment="e">
<m>y=\sqrt{1+x}</m>
</label>
<label at="bs-q1-lab" anchor="(-1.22,1.55)" alignment="e" color="#E69F00">
<m>1+\frac{x}{2}</m>
</label>
<label at="bs-q2-lab" anchor="(-1.22,1.32)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>1+\frac{x}{2}-\frac{x^2}{8}</m>
</label>
<label at="bs-q4-lab" anchor="(-1.22,1.09)" alignment="e" color="#009E73">
<m>\text{five terms}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The curve y equals the square root of one plus x, drawn as a dashed black line on the interval from negative one to one, together with three partial sums of its binomial series: the linear approximation in orange, the quadratic in blue, and the five term partial sum in green. All curves pass through zero comma one, and the five term sum is nearly indistinguishable from the function across the interval.">
  <annotation ref="bs-f" text="The function y equals the square root of one plus x, dashed black."/>
  <annotation ref="bs-q1" text="The two term partial sum, one plus x over two."/>
  <annotation ref="bs-q2" text="The three term partial sum, which adds the quadratic correction."/>
  <annotation ref="bs-q4" text="The five term partial sum, which is very close to the function on the whole interval."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</statement>
</example>
</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-binomial-applications">
<title>Applications</title>

<p>
Many integrals that arise in practice cannot be expressed in terms of
elementary functions. Replacing the integrand by its Taylor series and
integrating term by term produces a series for the integral, and the
partial sums of that series give numerical estimates whose accuracy we
can control. Taylor series also give an efficient way to evaluate limits
of indeterminate form, often faster than repeated applications of
l'H&#xf4;pital's rule.
</p>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-binomial-integral-estimate">
<title>Estimating a non-elementary integral</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use the binomial series to estimate

<md>
\int_0^{1/3} \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^3}} \, dx,
</md>

and compute an upper bound for the error of your estimate.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(The antiderivative of <m>\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^3}}</m> is not an
elementary function, so replace the integrand by its series: apply
<xref ref="eq-skel-binomial-series"/> with <m>m = -\frac12</m>,
substituting <m>-x^3</m> for <m>x</m>. Integrate the result term by
term, evaluate at the endpoints, and keep the first two terms as your
estimate. For the error, note that all the terms are positive, so the
error is exactly the sum of the omitted terms; show that each omitted
term is less than <m>\frac{1}{27}</m> times the one before it and
compare with a geometric series, using
<xref ref="eq-skel-series-geometric-sum"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,20);
\draw[gray!35] (0,14.5) -- (15,14.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9.5) -- (15,9.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,19.7) {series for $\left(1-x^3\right)^{-1/2}$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,14.2) {integrate term by term:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.2) {two-term estimate:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {upper bound for the error:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the binomial series of the integrand, the term by term integration, the two term numerical estimate, and the upper bound for the error.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-binomial-limit">
<title>Evaluating a limit</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use series to evaluate

<md>
\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{\sqrt{1 + \sin x} - 1 - \frac{x}{2}}{x^2}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Start from the binomial series for <m>\sqrt{1+u}</m> that you found in
<xref ref="ex-skel-binomial-fractional"/>. Near <m>x = 0</m> the
quantity <m>\sin x</m> is small, so substitute <m>u = \sin x</m>, then
replace <m>\sin x</m> by its Maclaurin series
<m>\sin x = x - \frac{x^3}{3!} + \cdots</m> and collect powers of
<m>x</m> up to <m>x^3</m>. Subtract <m>1 + \frac{x}{2}</m>, divide by
<m>x^2</m>, and take the limit. Which coefficient decides the answer?)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,18);
\draw[gray!35] (0,15) -- (15,15);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9) -- (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,5.5) -- (15,5.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,17.7) {$\sqrt{1+u} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,14.7) {$\sqrt{1+\sin x} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (5.00,14.7) {(collect powers of $x$)};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {numerator:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.2) {the limit:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the binomial series of the square root, the substituted and collected series, the numerator, and the value of the limit.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<p>
In the second example the constant terms and the <m>\frac{x}{2}</m>
terms cancel exactly, so the limit is decided by the coefficient of
<m>x^2</m> in the series for <m>\sqrt{1+\sin x}</m>. Evaluating the same
limit by l'H&#xf4;pital's rule would require differentiating the
numerator twice, a much longer computation.
</p>
</subsection>

</section>
